      "message": "perf(xtask): run release-gate suites in a longest-first queue; narrow the alone list\n\nThe release gate kept a 16-core server busy only during its builds: after\nthem ~80% of the run used one or two cores, because every app and kernel\nstep ran alone, one after another, and the remaining steps ran in barrier\nwaves that each waited for their slowest lane.\n\nA. lanes::alone() is narrowed. Builds (--no-run), clippy and every UI step\n   (windows) still run alone, as does an app or kernel step that is not one\n   named target. Of the app/kernel targets only lanes::ISOLATED runs alone:\n   the lib suites and the integration binaries with a wall-clock assertion\n   (import watchdogs on recv_timeout, deadline loops, elapsed() < limit),\n   found by grepping the suites. Every other app/kernel target joins the\n   shared lanes. A test pins that each listed binary exists.\n\nB. The barrier waves are replaced by a work queue (queue.rs): the builds\n   first, each alone; then budget::lane_limit lanes of budget::per_lane jobs,\n   each lane pulling the next step as it finishes; then the alone steps, one\n   at a time with the whole budget. The queue is ordered longest first\n   (Graham's LPT rule) by the step seconds the previous release run wrote to\n   gate-logs/release-*/timings.tsv (new: every release run writes one); a\n   step with no history ranks with the longest, and with no history at all\n   the queue keeps plan order. The checkout guard runs before every step, a\n   failed build still stops the run, a failed suite still stops nothing.\n\nC. Alone steps already get --test-threads=<budget> through budget::scale\n   (the plan's 6 is the default budget); the gate now prints a note when\n   the machine has more cores than the budget, since CAMCRAFT_GATE_JOBS is\n   what lets a 16-core runner use them.\n\n--dry-run still prints the whole plan, the queue in pull order.\n\nMeasurement plan: run `cargo xtask gate release --no-snapshots` on the same\nHetzner ccx43 (16 cores, CAMCRAFT_GATE_JOBS=16) at the base commit and at\nthis head, twice each at this head (the first run records timings.tsv, the\nsecond uses it), and compare wall time from the gate summary plus mean core\nuse (sar/mpstat) after the builds.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01CUsqDAAAJyAPrC37zBzbbX",
